From: PwndaWallet <pwndamining@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] wallet: expose importAddress, so a host can repair the
 engine's own address bookkeeping

The engine can hold funds it does not report.

`WalletManager.getCachedTotalBalance` sums `cached_balance` over rows already
present in `wallet_addresses`. It is not a chain scan, and `syncBalances`
only refreshes balances for addresses ALREADY in that table -- it never
discovers new ones. Nothing keeps the table complete with respect to the
chain, and the only repair command upstream ships cannot close the gap:
`"rescan"` -> `rescanWalletAddresses` -> `runMigration` derives a fixed
`num_addresses = 20` per chain and then forces `last_external_index` and
`last_internal_index` to 19. It can neither find nor record anything past
index 19.

So an address beyond that is unreachable by every existing endpoint, and its
balance is silently absent from every total the engine reports. Observed on
mainnet LTC, 2026-08-23: 4.02888049 LTC at internal index 20 -- put there by
the engine's own change allocation (see PWNDA-PATCH-9, which stops new change
landing there) -- missing entirely from `/json/wallets`, while an independent
scan of the same account over the same seed found it immediately.

`WalletManager.importAddress` already does exactly the right thing: derives
both chains to locate the index, inserts a real `wallet_addresses` row, and
advances `last_*_index` so future allocation cannot collide with it. It had no
caller outside internal input-signing.

This exposes it as `pwndaimportaddress` and adds no new capability of its own.
Note it must be `importAddress` and NOT the already-exposed `watchaddress`:
`importWatchOnlyAddress` writes to `wallet_watch_only`, and
`getCachedTotalBalance` excludes those rows unless they carry an encrypted
private key -- so importing this way would track the address and still never
count it, which is the more confusing failure of the two.

`max_scan_index` defaults to 200 rather than the function's own 1000: a host
that knows the address is scanning its own account, and 200 covers any
realistic gap-limit walk at a fraction of the derivation cost.

Not an upstream-PR candidate on its own -- it is a host-repair surface -- but
the gap it exists to repair (a rescan that cannot see past its own hardcoded
20) is upstream's, and worth reporting separately.

Marker: PWNDA-PATCH-12
---
--- a/basicswap/js_server.py
+++ b/basicswap/js_server.py
@@ -389,6 +389,42 @@
                     raise ValueError("WalletManager not available")
                 addresses = wm.getAllAddresses(coin_type)
                 return bytes(json.dumps({"addresses": addresses}), "UTF-8")
+            elif cmd == "pwndaimportaddress":
+                # PWNDA-PATCH-12: teach the engine about an address of its
+                # OWN account that its bookkeeping never recorded.
+                #
+                # getCachedTotalBalance sums cached_balance over rows already
+                # present in wallet_addresses; it is not a chain scan. Nothing
+                # guarantees that table stays complete, and the only repair
+                # command upstream ships -- "rescan" -> rescanWalletAddresses
+                # -> runMigration -- derives a fixed num_addresses=20 per chain
+                # and then forces last_*_index to 19, so it can neither find
+                # nor record anything past index 19. An address beyond that is
+                # unreachable by every existing endpoint, and its balance is
+                # silently missing from every total the engine reports.
+                # Observed on mainnet LTC 2026-08-23: 4.02888049 LTC at
+                # internal index 20, absent from /json/wallets entirely.
+                #
+                # WalletManager.importAddress already does exactly the right
+                # thing -- derives both chains to find the index, inserts a
+                # real wallet_addresses row (so it counts, unlike
+                # importWatchOnlyAddress, whose rows are excluded from the
+                # total unless they carry a private key), and advances
+                # last_*_index so future allocation cannot collide with it.
+                # It simply had no caller outside internal input-signing.
+                # This exposes it; it adds no new capability of its own.
+                post_data = getFormData(post_string, is_json)
+                address = get_data_entry(post_data, "address")
+                max_scan_index = int(
+                    get_data_entry_or(post_data, "max_scan_index", 200)
+                )
+                wm = swap_client.getWalletManager()
+                if wm is None:
+                    raise ValueError("WalletManager not available")
+                imported = wm.importAddress(coin_type, address, max_scan_index)
+                return bytes(
+                    json.dumps({"success": imported, "address": address}), "UTF-8"
+                )
             elif cmd == "fixseedid":
                 root_key = swap_client.getWalletKey(coin_type, 1)
                 swap_client.storeSeedIDForCoin(root_key, coin_type)
